<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why owners turn to home insurance appraisal in South Carolina</h2>
<p>Home insurance appraisal in South Carolina is a policy-based process for settling the dollar value of a covered loss. It fits best when the carrier agrees something happened and agrees it is covered, but the payment leaves you short. Instead of trading emails with a desk adjuster for months, each side names an appraiser and the numbers are worked out line by line.</p>
<p>We represent homeowners statewide: historic houses in Charleston and Beaufort, 1920s bungalows near Five Points in Columbia, mill-village cottages in the Upstate, manufactured homes in rural counties and new subdivision homes in Summerville, Fort Mill and Simpsonville.</p>
<h2>What a home appraisal covers</h2>
<h3>Dwelling and other structures</h3>
<p>The house itself, plus detached garages, fences, sheds and docks where the policy covers them. We measure roofs, walls and floors ourselves and write the repair the way a contractor would actually do it.</p>
<h3>Contents and additional living expense</h3>
<p>Personal property and the cost of living elsewhere during repairs can also be in dispute. Whether they fall within a particular appraisal depends on what the demand and policy say, so we confirm the scope at the start.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Older homes</b>Plaster walls, heart-pine floors, wavy glass and full-dimension framing don’t price like drywall and builder-grade trim. When a carrier estimate swaps in modern materials without saying so, that is often where much of the gap sits.</div>
<h2>Common estimate disputes on South Carolina homes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> A repaired section of floor, siding or cabinetry that won’t match the rest of a continuous run.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades.</strong> Items required by the local building department, and whether the policy’s ordinance or law coverage pays for them.</li>
<li><strong>Overhead and profit.</strong> Whether a general contractor is reasonably needed to coordinate roofing, drywall, paint, flooring and electrical work.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> How much is held back on an actual cash value basis and what it takes to recover it. Our <a href="/free-claim-tools/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV and ACV holdback calculator</a> shows how the numbers interact.</li>
<li><strong>Access and height.</strong> Elevated coastal homes and two-story Charleston single houses with piazzas need lifts, scaffolding or extra labor that a basic estimate may ignore.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Documentation that helps a home appraisal</h2>
<p>Gather the declarations page and full policy, every estimate and letter from the carrier, photos from before and after the loss, any receipts for tarps, water extraction or board-up, and contractor bids. If you have older photos from a real estate listing or a family event, they can show the condition of finishes before the damage.</p>
<p>A short written timeline also helps: when the damage happened, when you reported it, when the adjuster inspected and when payments arrived.</p>
<h2>What the process looks like for a homeowner</h2>
<p>After you or the carrier sends the written demand, we confirm our appointment, inspect the home and write our estimate. The carrier’s appraiser does the same. We then compare the two estimates line by line, agree on what we can and set aside the items that remain open. If an umpire is needed, both appraisers present their support on those open items only. You stay informed at each step, and nothing is signed without a clear explanation of what the award covers and what it leaves out.</p>
<h2>When appraisal isn’t the right tool for a homeowner</h2>
<p>Appraisal settles amounts, not coverage. If the carrier says the damage came from long-term leaks, settling, a flood the policy excludes or poor maintenance, the disagreement is about cause and coverage. Those disputes can be harder to fit into appraisal and may need a South Carolina attorney.</p>
<p>It also may not make sense when the gap is small compared with the cost of hiring an appraiser and sharing an umpire. We will talk through the numbers honestly before you commit, and if a complaint to the South Carolina Department of Insurance (SCDOI) or a simple supplement request is a better first step, we will say so.</p>

<h2>Questions about home insurance appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can I still use appraisal if I already cashed the insurance check?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often yes, because accepting an undisputed payment usually does not end the claim. Policy wording and any release you signed matter, so read carefully and ask a South Carolina attorney if you are unsure.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal cover my contents as well as the house?</summary><div class="a"><p>It can, if the demand and policy include personal property. We confirm the scope with the other appraiser at the start so nothing is left out.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will my premium go up because I asked for appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Appraisal is a right written into the policy. Renewal and pricing decisions depend on the carrier and state rules, and the SCDOI can answer questions about how insurers handle renewals.</p></div></details></div>
